# Accepted arbitration shortfalls — a CPU that will not reach every row it
# declares, and why it never will.
#
# Below 100% is not automatically wrong, but it must never be unexplained. A
# shortfall is one of two things and the number alone cannot tell them apart:
# a decision we have taken, or a debt we owe. This file is where a shortfall
# says which, and `cargo xtask coverage --check` holds it to that: a CPU below
# 100% with no entry here fails, and so does one whose shortfall grew past
# what it declares.
#
# The count is rows, not a percentage, so it does not drift when a spec gains
# forms. Widening one is a deliberate edit with a reason, like the stamp.
#
# A reason opening with `owed` means the opposite of settled: these rows are
# meant to come back. Such an entry still lets work merge — the alternative is
# blocking a merge on a growth run that needs the reference tools — but it
# stops a release. `cargo xtask coverage --no-debt` runs before the tag, and
# stays red until a growth run recovers the rows or the reason is rewritten as
# the decision it turned out to be. That bounds how long a de-arbitrated form
# can hide.
#
# CPU	rows	why
6809	3	`reset`, `rhf` and `hcf` are input-only: the form audit works through the disassembler, which by decision never writes them — decisions/undocumented-opcodes-are-input-only.md
8039	4	the ROM-less bus commits the four BUS-port ops, so the part forbids them and the audit skips them deliberately
